#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
tools/shim-census -- plan_shim.md Phase 1a: a consumer census taken over the
LINK LINE, never over the objects directory.

    tools/shim-census who-imports src/game/shared/util_shared.cpp
    tools/shim-census who-imports _Z11UTIL_Removev
    tools/shim-census orphans
    tools/shim-census orphans --side server
    tools/shim-census passthrough
    tools/shim-census --check src/sourcesharp/game/shared/handle_cross.h
    tools/shim-census --self-test

=============================================================================
WHY THIS EXISTS
=============================================================================

An objects directory is NOT the link line.  Under VPC it was
`src/_vpc_/ninja/debug/objs/<side>_sourcesharp/`; under CMake (Phase 6b) it is
`farm/<config>/build/**/CMakeFiles/<side>_sourcesharp.dir/`, and it is the
same trap: ninja keeps the .o of a TU whose edge went away.  It is
every object the build has ever produced in this tree, including objects of
translation units the build has since evicted.  On 2026-09-19 that directory
held 190 server objects for a graph that links 144, and 327 client objects for
a graph that links 272.  The 46 + 55 leftovers (baseentity.o, entitylist.o,
gamemovement.o, util.o, gib.o, c_baseentity.o, cdll_client_int.o, ... all dated
09-03/09-05) were entity-port casualties that nothing links any more.

Every consumer count taken over that directory was wrong, and wrong in the
direction that blocks a deletion:

    game/server/mapentities.cpp        13 consumers by directory,  6 on the graph
    game/shared/util_shared.cpp         7 consumers by directory,  3 on the graph
    game/shared/gamestringpool.cpp     18 consumers by directory,  7 on the graph
    game/shared/predictableid.cpp       3 consumers by directory,  1 on the graph
    game/server/AI_Criteria.cpp         2 consumers by directory,  0 on the graph

That last row is the shape that matters: a file with zero live consumers reads
as "still used" and survives a deletion pass it should not have survived.

So this tool enumerates objects from the .so's LINK EDGE in the CMake build's
build.ninja, maps each to its source through compile_commands.json, and
refuses a compile database that disagrees with the CMakeLists source lists
(tools/cmakegraph.py).  It REFUSES to answer when it cannot trust what it is
reading.  A census that cannot measure must fail, not quietly answer
(memory: a-check-log-has-no-provenance).

=============================================================================
THE THREE METHOD RULES
=============================================================================

 1. OBJECTS COME FROM THE GRAPH.  `ls` over the objects directory is the bug
    above; `ld` does not reliably name the objects it consumed (memory:
    ld-stops-naming-objects) so linker output is a floor, not a census.  The
    link edge's inputs in build.ninja are exact -- they are what the build
    links.  Objects are printed under VPC's flat name (x.cpp.o -> x.o), so
    rows read the same as every census taken before the move to CMake.

 2. `nm` COUNTS FILTER ON SYMBOL TYPE.  Definitions are `nm -C --defined-only`
    restricted to types [TWDBRV]; undefined references are `nm -C -u` (type U).
    A plain `nm` without the type filter reads weak inline emissions and
    undefined rows as definitions and every count inflates (memory:
    nm-without-symbol-type-inflates).  V is IN the filter on purpose: lane C2
    read 0 consumers for shell_explosion_overlay.cpp with a [TWDBR] filter
    because its only edge is `vtable for CExplosionOverlay`, which is type V.

 3. SYMBOLS ARE JOINED MANGLED.  `nm -C` demangles defined and undefined rows
    inconsistently on the same object, which silently loses consumers -- it
    lost six of seven cdll_util.cpp edges for tools/7f-consumers.  So the join
    runs on the mangled name and `-C` is used only to DISPLAY.  (Both readings
    are taken; --show-demangled prints the human one.)

=============================================================================
THE REFUSALS, AND ONE PLACE THIS TOOL CONTRADICTS ITS OWN BRIEF
=============================================================================

Fatal, always:

  UNREADABLE  build.ninja or compile_commands.json is missing, has no link
            edge for a side, or disagrees with the CMakeLists source list (a
            list edited and the build not re-run).  tools/cmakegraph.py.
  MISSING   the graph names an object that is not on disk.  nm cannot read it,
            so every count that object would have contributed is silently 0.
  STALE     an object is OLDER than the source it is compiled from: the source
            was edited and the build was not re-run.

Reported, not fatal:

  GHOST     an object on disk in one of the two directories that the graph does
            not name.  This tool never reads it -- that is the whole point --
            but its presence is exactly the condition that made every
            directory-based count in the analysis wave wrong, so it is named
            and counted in the header of every run.  `--strict` promotes it to
            fatal.

Phase 1a's brief asks for a third refusal: "refuse if any object on disk is
NEWER than the ninja file".  Measured, that rule fires on every successful
build and always will: the generator writes the ninja file, then ninja
compiles, so every object is newer than the graph by construction.  Under VPC,
2026-09-19 --

    sdk_csharp_debug.ninja        2026-09-19 20:15:33
    oldest object (memoverride.o) 2026-09-19 20:15:45
    newest object (util_shared.o) 2026-09-19 20:15:51

and under CMake, 2026-09-27: build.ninja 19:26:02, the oldest server object
19:26:04.  A refusal that fires on every correct tree is not an instrument.
The literal rule is kept as `--mtime-policy objects-older`, so nobody has to
take this on trust: run it and watch it refuse a tree that is perfectly good.

Until Phase 6b the DEFAULT was the opposite comparison, `graph-newer`: refuse
when the ninja file is newer than an object it names.  That one fails on good
trees too, and under CMake it fails after EVERY source-list edit: the
re-configure rewrites build.ninja and ninja recompiles only what changed, so
every untouched object is older than the graph (measured on a two-file CMake
project: add b.cpp to the list, rebuild, and a.cpp.o is a second older than
build.ninja).  On the last VPC-built farm (main, 8e35279) it refused 107 of
108 server and all 253 client objects.  It is kept as `--mtime-policy graph-newer`; the default is
now `source-newer` (STALE above), with the list/database cross-check covering
what `graph-newer` was for.

=============================================================================
PASSTHROUGH (plan_shim.md step 3g) -- A REPORT, NOT AN ACTION
=============================================================================

`passthrough` classifies every SourceSharp_* entry point body in the shim.  A
slot is PASSTHROUGH when all three hold:

  * every parameter is blittable (integers, float/double, bool, char, enums of
    those, and pointers to them; NOT a C++ reference, NOT a by-value class
    type, NOT varargs);
  * the body makes exactly ONE call through an engine global (engine->,
    filesystem->, modelinfo->, enginesound->, enginetrace->,
    networkstringtable->, debugoverlay->, soundemitterbase->, physenv->,
    physcollision->, gameeventmanager->, staticpropmgr->);
  * the body touches no shell, handle or slot-table state.

A body that FIRST resolves a token, handle or entity index and only then
reaches the engine is NOT a passthrough -- that resolution is the handle state
3g excludes -- so two further checks apply: the body may contain no call other
than the engine one (bar a named list of diagnostics: SOURCESHARP_TRACE,
Assert, Msg and friends) and no `->` whose receiver is not an engine global.

A third verdict, NEAR-PASSTHROUGH, is for bodies that are otherwise strict but
copy the engine's answer into a caller-owned buffer with a named marshalling
call (V_strncpy, V_snprintf, V_FileBase, memcpy, ...). They are reported
separately rather than folded into either side, because a bound vtable does
that copy for free and the 3g reader wants both numbers.

HOW I KNOW THE NUMBER IS NOT AN ARTEFACT OF THE REGEX.  A crude awk over the
tree read 13 of ~800 bodies.  This one reads, at 216533fa8:

    925  SourceSharp_* definitions found under src/sourcesharp
      0  bodies that did not brace-balance
    476  engineApi.* = SourceSharp_*; assignments in the slot table
      0  of those assignments whose body the scanner failed to find
      4  strictly passthrough   (1 of them in 3g's entity/physics carve-out)
      2  near-passthrough

Four things make that checkable rather than believable:

  a) THE CLASSIFICATION IS A PARTITION.  Every entry point lands in exactly one
     bucket -- a verdict, or one named rejection reason -- and the report
     prints the bucket sizes and asserts they sum to the population.  A regex
     that quietly matches nothing cannot hide inside a partition; it shows up
     as a bucket that swallowed everything.  The largest bucket here is "no
     engine-global call" at 798, which is the bucket that SHOULD be largest:
     most of the ABI is entity, physics and networking ops that never touch an
     engine interface directly.  If the passthrough regex were broken, that
     bucket would be 925.

  b) BODIES ARE BRACE-BALANCED, NOT LINE-MATCHED.  A definition is found by its
     signature and then read to its matching close brace with a scanner that
     strips comments, string and char literals first.  A body that does not
     balance is not guessed at: it is counted as `unparsed` and named, and the
     report says in its own header that the count is untrustworthy if that
     number is not zero.

  c) THE POPULATION IS CROSS-CHECKED AGAINST THE SLOT TABLE.  Every
     `engineApi.X = SourceSharp_Y;` assignment in sourcesharp_gamesystem.cpp is
     joined to the bodies found.  An assignment whose body the scanner did not
     find is reported as `unresolved` -- the direct test for "the signature
     regex missed real bodies" -- and it is printed whether it is zero or not.

  d) THE FALSE POSITIVES WERE FOUND BY READING THEM.  The first version of this
     classifier answered 25, and `passthrough --explain` on two of those 25
     showed both were wrong: SourceSharp_AreaPortalSetOpen resolved a token and
     wrote pPortal->m_bOpen before calling the engine, and
     SourceSharp_ClientEntityModelType resolved an entity from an index and
     called a virtual on it.  Checks 4 and 5 above exist because of those two,
     and --self-test pins both shapes so they cannot come back.  A count that
     has never been read row by row is a count nobody has checked.

  `passthrough --explain <SourceSharp_Name>` prints the verdict for one entry
  point with the evidence lines and the body as the classifier saw it, so any
  row is hand-checkable in about ten seconds.  Use it before believing the
  total.

